  "intro": "Grammar rules make writing clear. A noun names a person, place, or thing; a pronoun replaces a noun (he, she, it, they); a verb is an action or being word; an adjective describes a noun; and an adverb tells how, when, or where (often ending in -ly). Verbs change for the past (walk to walked). Third graders use these correctly.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Which word is an adjective in 'The fluffy cat slept'?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "An adjective describes a noun.",
          "'Fluffy' describes the cat.",
          "So 'fluffy' is the adjective."
        ],
        "answer": "Fluffy."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Replace the noun with a pronoun: 'Maria is nice.' (use she/he/it)",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "A pronoun replaces a noun.",
          "'Maria' is a girl, so use 'she.'",
          "So: 'She is nice.'"
        ],
        "answer": "She is nice."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Change the verb to past tense: 'Today I jump. Yesterday I ____.'",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "The past tense of 'jump' adds -ed.",
          "So yesterday I 'jumped.'"
        ],
        "answer": "Jumped."
      }
